    "description": "PENDING FUNCTIONAL EQUIVALENCE REVIEW FOR 'HASHBYTES' NODE",
    "problem_description": "The code calls HASHBYTES to compute a hash, commonly to build a change-detection or reconciliation key across several columns. The converted call needs review because the algorithm names, the input encoding and the return type differ in Snowflake, so the hash values will not match those produced by SQL Server unless the call is constructed carefully.",
    "recommended_fix": "Choose the Snowflake function that matches the algorithm and fix the encoding. For HASHBYTES('SHA2_256', x) use SHA2(x, 256), which returns a lowercase hex VARCHAR, or SHA2_BINARY(x, 256) for the raw bytes that HASHBYTES returned; for MD5 use MD5 or MD5_BINARY. Then make the input identical: SQL Server hashes the bytes of the value, so an NVARCHAR input was hashed as UTF-16LE, while Snowflake hashes UTF-8, which is the usual reason values differ. Normalise explicitly by casting every component to VARCHAR, applying a fixed NULL replacement such as NVL(col, ''), and concatenating with a delimiter that cannot occur in the data. If stored hashes must stay comparable with values computed on SQL Server, recompute the whole column in Snowflake rather than mixing the two. Where the hash is only used to detect change within Snowflake, HASH() is cheaper, but it is not portable and must not be persisted for cross-system comparison."
