  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-0073",
    "description": "PENDING FUNCTIONAL EQUIVALENCE REVIEW FOR 'NEWSEQUENTIALID' NODE",
    "problem_description": "A column defaults to NEWSEQUENTIALID(), which in SQL Server generates a GUID that increases monotonically within the machine's run, chosen specifically so that clustered-index inserts stay sequential. Snowflake has no NEWSEQUENTIALID and no clustered index, so neither the exact value semantics nor the ordering purpose carries over.",
    "recommended_fix": "Decide which property the column actually needs. If it only needs a unique identifier, default it to UUID_STRING(), which produces a random RFC 4122 UUID as VARCHAR(36); the loss of monotonicity is harmless in Snowflake because there is no clustered index whose fragmentation it was protecting. If downstream logic depends on the ordering of the generated values, do not emulate the GUID ordering: add a separate ordering column with an explicit sequence or IDENTITY, or order by the row's load timestamp, and keep the UUID purely as an identifier. If the GUIDs must match values already generated in SQL Server, migrate the existing values as data rather than regenerating them, and apply the new default only to rows inserted after cutover."
