  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-0073",
    "description": "PENDING FUNCTIONAL EQUIVALENCE REVIEW FOR 'SetLocalVariableStatement' NODE",
    "problem_description": "A SET statement assigning a local variable was converted but flagged for review, because the assignment's source expression or its no-row behaviour may not be equivalent. Common causes are SET @v = (SELECT ...) where the subquery can return no rows, an assignment that relies on T-SQL implicit conversion, or a compound assignment such as SET @v += expr.",
    "recommended_fix": "Make the assignment's semantics explicit. For an assignment from a query, use SELECT expr INTO :v FROM ... and handle the no-row case deliberately: T-SQL leaves the variable unchanged, and a subquery assignment yields NULL, whereas Snowflake Scripting raises an error if no row is returned, so wrap it or supply a default with a MAX or NVL aggregate that always produces one row. For compound assignment, expand it to v := :v + expr. For assignments that depended on implicit conversion, add an explicit CAST to the declared type, because Snowflake's conversion rules differ, particularly between numeric and VARCHAR and in date parsing. Verify the variable's declared precision and scale, since silent truncation in SQL Server can become an error in Snowflake."
