  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0041",
    "description": "XML TYPE METHOD nodes IS NOT SUPPORTED IN SNOWFLAKE",
    "problem_description": "The source shreds an XML document into rows with the .nodes('xpath') method, used with CROSS APPLY to produce one row per matching element so that each element's values can then be read. Snowflake has no .nodes() method, so the row-generating step of the shred is missing after conversion.",
    "recommended_fix": "Replace .nodes() with LATERAL FLATTEN over the parsed document. Parse once with PARSE_XML(col), reach the repeating element with XMLGET, and flatten its children: FROM t, LATERAL FLATTEN(input => XMLGET(PARSE_XML(t.col), 'Targets'):\"$\") f produces one row per child element, with f.value standing in for the per-node reference that .nodes() yielded. Inside the projection, read each field from f.value with XMLGET(f.value, 'FieldName'):\"$\" and cast it explicitly, which replaces the .value() calls that followed the original CROSS APPLY. Where the XPath selected a deeper or filtered node set, flatten the outer level and apply the filter as a WHERE predicate rather than encoding it in a path expression."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0041",
    "description": "XML TYPE METHOD query IS NOT SUPPORTED IN SNOWFLAKE",
    "problem_description": "The source extracts an XML fragment with the .query('xpath') method, which returns XML rather than a scalar, typically to pass a subtree onward or to re-serialise part of a document. Snowflake has no XML type and no .query() method, so the fragment extraction has no direct counterpart.",
    "recommended_fix": "Work on the parsed VARIANT and return the subtree from it. Use XMLGET(PARSE_XML(col), 'Element') to obtain the element as a VARIANT, which is the fragment .query() would have returned; chain XMLGET for a deeper path and pass the instance index for a specific occurrence. If the consumer needs XML text rather than a VARIANT, serialise with TO_VARCHAR or TO_JSON depending on the format the consumer accepts, and confirm whether it can accept JSON, which is usually the better target in Snowflake. Where .query() was used to build a new document by projecting elements, construct the result explicitly with OBJECT_CONSTRUCT and ARRAY_AGG over flattened nodes rather than attempting an XPath-shaped rewrite."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0075",
    "description": "TRANSLATION FOR BUILT-IN PROCEDURE 'sp_releaseapplock' IS NOT CURRENTLY SUPPORTED.",
    "problem_description": "The procedure releases an application-level mutex taken earlier with sp_getapplock. Snowflake has no application lock manager, so this call has no equivalent and the release half of the locking protocol is lost along with the acquire half.",
    "recommended_fix": "Replace it with the release step of whatever mechanism replaced sp_getapplock. If a lock table was introduced, release the lock by clearing the row, with UPDATE lock_table SET holder = NULL, acquired_at = NULL WHERE lock_name = '...', and place that statement where sp_releaseapplock was plus in the procedure's EXCEPTION block, so an error cannot leave the lock held. Store the acquiring session with CURRENT_SESSION() and the acquisition time so a stale lock can be identified and broken after a crash, which sp_releaseapplock did not need because SQL Server released locks on disconnect. If serialisation was instead moved to a single Snowflake Task, delete this call outright, since the Task's non-overlapping execution needs no release."
