  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0075",
    "description": "TRANSLATION FOR BUILT-IN PROCEDURE 'sp_xml_preparedocument' IS NOT CURRENTLY SUPPORTED.",
    "problem_description": "The procedure parses an XML document into an in-memory handle with sp_xml_preparedocument, so that OPENXML can later shred it by handle. Snowflake has no document handle model and no OPENXML, so neither the preparation step nor the handle it returns can be reproduced.",
    "recommended_fix": "Remove the prepare step entirely and parse the document inline where it is read. Replace the handle with the parsed value itself: PARSE_XML(xml_text) yields a VARIANT that can be navigated directly, so the variable that held the integer handle should be redeclared as VARIANT and assigned the parsed document, or the parse can be inlined at the point of use. Then rewrite the OPENXML that consumed the handle as a LATERAL FLATTEN over the parsed value, as described for the OPENXML issue. Delete the matching sp_xml_removedocument call at the same time, since there is no handle to free. This usually simplifies the procedure, because the prepare, shred and remove sequence collapses into one query."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0075",
    "description": "TRANSLATION FOR BUILT-IN PROCEDURE 'sp_xml_removedocument' IS NOT CURRENTLY SUPPORTED.",
    "problem_description": "The procedure frees the in-memory XML document previously created by sp_xml_preparedocument. Snowflake has no document handles to free, so the call has no equivalent; it is cleanup for a mechanism that does not exist after conversion.",
    "recommended_fix": "Delete the call. Once sp_xml_preparedocument has been replaced by PARSE_XML and the OPENXML shred by LATERAL FLATTEN, there is no handle and no resource to release, and Snowflake needs no explicit cleanup for the parsed VARIANT. Make sure the deletion is done together with the prepare and OPENXML rewrites rather than on its own, and check whether the call sat in an error-handling path: in the source it may have been the only cleanup in a CATCH block, so removing it should not remove any logging or rollback that shared that block."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0082",
    "description": "CROSS APPLY HAS BEEN CONVERTED TO LEFT OUTER JOIN AND REQUIRES MANUAL VALIDATION.",
    "problem_description": "A CROSS APPLY was rewritten as a LEFT OUTER JOIN. These are not equivalent: CROSS APPLY discards outer rows whose correlated subquery produces no rows, while a LEFT OUTER JOIN keeps them with NULLs in the applied columns. The converted query can therefore return more rows than the source, and aggregates or NOT NULL assumptions over the applied columns can change.",
    "recommended_fix": "Decide which semantics the query needs and make it explicit. To preserve CROSS APPLY behaviour exactly, use an inner lateral join: FROM t, LATERAL (subquery) s, or INNER JOIN LATERAL (subquery) s ON TRUE, which drops outer rows with no match just as CROSS APPLY did. Keep the generated LEFT OUTER JOIN only where the applied subquery is known to always return at least one row, or where keeping the unmatched outer rows is actually wanted, which is OUTER APPLY semantics and should then use LEFT JOIN LATERAL. Validate by comparing row counts between source and target for the same input, and check any COUNT or SUM over the applied columns, since added NULL rows change COUNT(*) but not COUNT(col)."
