    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0086",
    "description": "OPENQUERY NODE NEEDS TO BE CHECKED.",
    "problem_description": "The code uses OPENQUERY to send a pass-through statement to a linked server, reading data from a system outside the database. Snowflake has no linked servers and no OPENQUERY, so the remote read cannot be expressed in SQL and the converted statement will not retrieve the external data.",
    "recommended_fix": "Replace the remote read with an ingestion path, chosen by what the linked server was. If the remote system is a file-producing source, land the data in cloud storage and read it through an external stage with COPY INTO or an external table, then query that instead of OPENQUERY. If it is another database, bring the data in with the data-migration tooling on a schedule, or expose it through a Snowflake data share if it is another Snowflake account, which removes the copy entirely. If the call was reaching out for a small lookup set, the simplest fix is to migrate that lookup table into Snowflake and join to it locally. Do not attempt to emulate pass-through querying with an external function unless the remote side genuinely needs per-row invocation, since that is slow and changes the failure model."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0094",
    "description": "WAITFOR DELAY WITH VARIABLE ':WAITTIME' WAS CONVERTED TO SYSTEM$WAIT, BUT THE VARIABLE MAY CONTAIN A TIME STRING IN 'HH:MM:SS' FORMAT. SYSTEM$WAIT EXPECTS A NUMERIC VALUE IN SECONDS.",
    "problem_description": "A WAITFOR DELAY whose interval comes from a variable was converted to SYSTEM$WAIT. The two take different arguments: WAITFOR DELAY accepts a time string such as '00:00:05', whereas SYSTEM$WAIT takes a number of seconds. If the variable still holds a time string, the call will fail or wait for the wrong duration.",
    "recommended_fix": "Convert the variable to seconds at the call site, or change its type. The direct fix is to compute seconds from the time string: SYSTEM$WAIT(DATEDIFF('second', '00:00:00'::TIME, :waittime::TIME)) when the variable keeps the HH:MM:SS format, or simply redeclare the variable as a number of seconds and set it accordingly, which is clearer. Pass the unit explicitly with SYSTEM$WAIT(n, 'SECONDS') so the intent is visible. Separately, question whether the wait is needed at all: these delays are usually throttling added to protect a SQL Server instance from lock contention, and in Snowflake they only extend warehouse run time and cost, so removing the throttle is often the right outcome once the surrounding loop is made set-based."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0115",
    "description": "T-SQL SESSION GLOBAL '@@TRANCOUNT' HAS NO SNOWFLAKE EQUIVALENT. REPLACE IT WITH EXPLICIT STATE OR REDESIGN THE DEPENDENT LOGIC.",
    "problem_description": "The procedure reads @@TRANCOUNT to find out how many transactions are open, normally to decide whether to begin a transaction or whether a rollback is safe. Snowflake has no nested transactions and no transaction counter, so there is no value to read and the conditional logic built on it cannot work as written.",
    "recommended_fix": "Remove the counter and make transaction ownership explicit instead. Decide for each procedure whether it owns its transaction or expects the caller to: if it owns it, open with BEGIN TRANSACTION unconditionally and commit or roll back on every exit path; if the caller owns it, do not open or roll back anything and let the error propagate. Replace tests such as IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK with a ROLLBACK inside an EXCEPTION block in the owning procedure only. Where the procedure genuinely must know whether a transaction is active, CURRENT_TRANSACTION() returns a transaction id when one is open and NULL otherwise, so a NULL test replaces the greater-than-zero test, but do not use it to emulate nesting depth, which does not exist."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0133",
    "description": "RESULT SET DELIVERY FROM 'SqlWithCte' WAS NOT PRESERVED. THE ROUTINE WAS CONVERTED, BUT THIS SOURCE CONSTRUCT CAN RETURN ROWS THAT THE GENERATED SNOWFLAKE ROUTINE DOES NOT FORWARD TO ITS CALLER.",
    "problem_description": "The procedure ends with, or contains, a query with a CTE whose rows were returned to the caller as an implicit result set, which T-SQL does automatically for any unassigned SELECT. The converted Snowflake procedure executes the query but does not forward its rows, so callers that consumed that result set receive nothing.",
    "recommended_fix": "Return the rows explicitly with a RESULTSET. Declare the procedure as RETURNS TABLE(...) with the query's column list and types, assign the query to a RESULTSET variable with res := (WITH ... SELECT ...), and finish with RETURN TABLE(res). Where more than one implicit result set was returned, Snowflake can forward only one, so decide which is the real output and write the others into tables the caller reads separately, or split the procedure. Where the rows were consumed by an application, confirm the client reads a returned table rather than a result set. If the query is purely diagnostic, consider inserting it into a log table instead of returning it, which removes the dependency on result-set delivery altogether."
  },
  {
    "code": "SSC-EWI-TS0204",
    "description": "PENDING SNOWCONVERT AI TRANSLATION FOR A MULTI-STATEMENT TABLE-VALUED FUNCTION BODY THAT CANNOT BE EXPRESSED AS ONE QUERY: a table variable with a computed column or a table constraint. THE FUNCTION BODY WAS NOT GENERATED.",
    "problem_description": "The object is a multi-statement table-valued function whose body builds up a table variable over several statements, and that table variable declares a computed column or a constraint. A Snowflake table UDF must be a single query, so this body cannot be translated mechanically and no function body was emitted at all, leaving the object unusable.",
    "recommended_fix": "Rewrite the body by hand, choosing between a single-query UDF and a procedure. Where the multi-statement logic is really a recursive walk, as hierarchy explosion usually is, it maps directly to one query: use a recursive CTE with WITH RECURSIVE over the parent-child table and return it from a table UDF declared RETURNS TABLE(...), which replaces the whole loop. Express the computed column as an expression in the SELECT list, and drop the table-variable constraint, enforcing it instead with a QUALIFY or a WHERE predicate that guarantees the same property. Where the body genuinely needs several steps, convert the object to a stored procedure that writes into a temporary table and returns it via RESULTSET, then update every caller, since a procedure cannot be used in a FROM clause the way the TVF was."
  },
